>David Damerell  <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
>>begin  quoting  Byron A Jeff <byron@cc.gatech.edu>:
>>>But that's only as long as you're don't have the protection AC. As soon as
>>>you get it, you start to play normally, and as we all know it only takes
>>>about 400-500 turns to go from XL1 to XL7 or so with the addition HPs you
>>>get at each gain in XL.
>>You still have to recover back to DL 1 or thereabouts.
>Why?

That does seem to be generally recommended; and - if you left some levels
partly unexplored - it would seem sensible to explore at the shallowest
available depth.

>At that point you have a XL9 pet with 72HP, an AC near -10 which
>both reduces the ToHit and reduces the damage if you are actually hit,

This is _not_ guaranteed. On any given hit, you might have AC -1 and only
1 point of damage reduction. This is why Kevin says a lucky hit can still
off you.

>>All we're saying is that, for a given level of playing skill, the
>>odds of that happening don't compare favourably with the odds of getting
>>an equally advanced character through normal play.
>An equally advanced character with 9 points less of AC under similar
>circumstances.

I don't mean that. I mean a character with the same overall odds of
ascension; probably with a worse AC then a racketeer, but with more other
stuff.

>We all of course play differently.

This does not preclude discussion of what playing style is best.

>My early to midgame goals are typically (in order):
>1) AC down to about -20 including all protections, armor, and enchantments.

See, this is way too much for stuff like Sokoban. Even a modest negative
AC provides good protection - provided you _have_ the max HP to soak the
odd lucky hit. 

>I learned long ago that AC and weight are the twin killers of early
>characters.

AC is certainly extremely valuable, but weight - not really. Effective
caching of found items is the key here.

>The protection racket significantly impacts my top two goals.

_If_ you survive it!

Again, no-one's saying the position isn't good once you've made those
donations and started to get some XL and HP. But you have to get there.

>But when I use to just go whacking in the early game, I found that both my
>pet, who was weaker, and myself, who was facing stronger monsters earlier,
>were both getting waxed by the RNG on the regular.

But we know for a fact that good "just whackers" - who, of course don't
just whack, but use devilish ingenuity to deal with whatever comes up (or,
yes, just play Valkyries) have a pretty good hit rate at getting to the
middle game. I've yet to see any racketeer claim as good a rate.

I'd like to test this; the trouble is, I can promise to play my best at
the racket, and a racketeer can promise to play their best conventional
game, but neither of us has the experience.

It's a shame the tournament does not record experience levels; otherwise
we could see, of the players with a high ascension hit rate, whose deaths
are often XL 1, DL lots.
